roke sweeping from the attacker territory toward the defender, partially drawn with energetic wet-brush taper. The same attacker and defender units remain standing.\n2. Caption: “2 · Dice land”. Show a small understated dark indigo dice tray integrated into the board; ivory dice have just settled inside it. Preserve the same attacker and defender and the same cropped territories. Gold appears only as a restrained active accent consistent with the interface.\n3. Caption: “3 · Defender falls”. The defending sumi-e unit dissolves into wisps and drifting flecks of silver-ivory ink smoke, visibly vanishing from its territory. The attacker remains poised. The effect is elegant and unmistakable, not violent or graphic.\n4. Caption: “4 · Ink floods”. The conquered defender territory changes ownership as the attacker’s muted color floods outward from the shared border in a spreading wet ink wash with visible capillary feathering. The attacker’s unit steps across the border into the conquered land; imply motion with one restrained dry-brush echo, while keeping the figure legible.\n\nAcross the bottom or integrated consistently beneath the panels, include the reference’s thin gold horizontal rule with a single brushed ensō centered on it, plus one understated rounded pill control with only its active element edged/glowing softly in gold. Keep these elements secondary to the storyboard and do not add extra interface labels unless essential.\n\nTypography must be accurate and fully legible. Preserve generous negative space. Make all four stages immediately understandable at a glance. No photorealism, no 3D rendering, no modern neon, no crowded UI, no extra captions beyond the four specified captions.",
